# Settings for this IDAH install, read by docker compose.
#
# Before installing: copy this file to .env, change what you need, then run
# ./install.sh. It keeps every value you set and generates the empty secrets.
# Without a .env it creates one from this file, as is.
#
# After installing: change a value, then apply it with docker compose up -d.
# Only the services that use it restart. Two changes need more than that, and
# README.md covers both: a new IDAH_VERSION (./install.sh --upgrade, which runs
# the migrations) and moving to your own PostgreSQL (./install.sh --provision,
# which copies the data over).
#
# Values with special characters: single-quote them, and every character is
# taken literally ('...'). A value cannot contain a single quote itself.

# === Docker Compose ========================================================
# The project this install owns: the prefix of every container and volume name
# it creates. install.sh fills it in from the directory's name, so renaming the
# directory later cannot detach the volumes. Two installs on one machine need
# different values.

# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=idah

# === Release ===============================================================

# The release to run. Every service uses this tag; upgrade by changing it.
# Empty: install.sh fills in its own release.
IDAH_VERSION=

# Images built from source rather than the published ones, e.g. idah-.
# IDAH_IMAGE_PREFIX=idah-

# === Web access ============================================================

# The public URL users open in a browser, scheme included, no trailing slash.
# Empty: install.sh asks.
IDAH_URL=

# The port nginx listens on, over plain HTTP: IDAH serves no HTTPS itself, so
# put your own TLS terminator in front of it and set IDAH_URL to its https://
# address. See "Serving HTTPS" in README.md.
# IDAH_HTTP_PORT=8080

# The address that port is published on. The default accepts connections from
# anywhere; 127.0.0.1 accepts them only from this machine, which is what you
# want when the TLS terminator runs here too, so nobody can reach IDAH over
# plain HTTP by going around it.
# IDAH_HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1

# HTTPS from your own certificate, served by IDAH's own nginx: put the
# certificate at config/certs/idah.crt and its key at config/certs/idah.key,
# then set this to ./config/nginx/tls.conf. Leave it unset when something in
# front of IDAH terminates TLS. That file documents both certificates; point
# this at a copy of your own to change how nginx serves TLS.
# IDAH_TLS_CONF=./config/nginx/tls.conf

# The HTTPS port and the address it is published on. It answers only when
# IDAH_TLS_CONF is set. 443 needs no extra privilege in Docker.
# IDAH_HTTPS_PORT=8443
# IDAH_HTTPS_BIND=127.0.0.1

# === Database ==============================================================
# Can also be changed after the install: set these, then ./install.sh
# --provision, which sets the new server up and copies the data over.
#
# By default IDAH runs its own PostgreSQL, and install.sh generates the
# password. Changing the credentials afterwards does not change that database:
# its volume keeps the ones it was created with.

POSTGRES_USER=idah
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=''

# Your own PostgreSQL: set POSTGRES_HOST, and POSTGRES_USER and
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD above to an existing user. install.sh then turns off the
# bundled database (IDAH_POSTGRES_CONTAINER=0) and checks the connection. See
# README.md for what the user needs. For a server on this machine use
# host.docker.internal: inside a container, localhost is the container itself.
#
# POSTGRES_SSLMODE: require (the default with your own server) encrypts without
# needing a CA. verify-full also checks the server's identity and needs
# IDAH_CA_CERT below. disable for a server without TLS.

# POSTGRES_HOST=db.example.com
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# POSTGRES_SSLMODE=require
# IDAH_POSTGRES_CONTAINER=0

# === Redis =================================================================
# Can also be changed after the install: nothing needs copying, but events in
# flight are lost, so do it while the system is quiet.
#
# By default IDAH runs its own. Every service uses the same one: it carries the
# events they send each other.
#
# Your own Redis: set REDIS_URL. install.sh then turns off the bundled Redis
# (IDAH_REDIS_CONTAINER=0) and checks the connection. redis:// is plain,
# rediss:// connects with TLS and verifies the server's certificate. The
# password is part of the URL: any character other than letters, digits and
# - . _ ~ must be percent-encoded, which ./install.sh --encode does. For a
# server on this machine use host.docker.internal.

# REDIS_URL=rediss://:password@redis.example.com:6379/0
# IDAH_REDIS_CONTAINER=0

# === Certificate authority =================================================
# For TLS to your own PostgreSQL or Redis when their certificates are not signed
# by a public CA: put the CA certificates (PEM, one file, several allowed) at
# config/certs/ca.pem, which the services see as /certs/ca.pem, and set this.
# PostgreSQL then trusts only this file, so it must include the CA of that
# server; everything else keeps the public CAs too. Required for
# POSTGRES_SSLMODE=verify-ca or verify-full.

# IDAH_CA_CERT=/certs/ca.pem

# === Service accounts ======================================================
# Generated by install.sh. Each internal service signs in to the others with
# its own account; these are their passwords. Changing one here does not change
# the account.

IDAH_SERVICE_PASSWORD_IAM=
IDAH_SERVICE_PASSWORD_DATASET=
IDAH_SERVICE_PASSWORD_MEDIA=
IDAH_SERVICE_PASSWORD_SETTING=
IDAH_SERVICE_PASSWORD_NOTIFICATION=
IDAH_SERVICE_PASSWORD_SYNC=
IDAH_SERVICE_PASSWORD_AUDIT=

# How long a service waits on another, in seconds: to connect, and for each
# read. A large file still streams as long as data keeps arriving.
# IDAH_API_OPEN_TIMEOUT=5
# IDAH_API_READ_TIMEOUT=30

# === Email =================================================================
# Off unless these are set. Uncomment them to send notifications; MAIL_SMTP_USER
# may stay empty for servers that need no authentication.

# MAIL_SMTP_HOST=mail.example.com
# MAIL_SMTP_PORT=587
# MAIL_SMTP_USER=''
# MAIL_SMTP_PASSWORD=''

# === File storage ==========================================================
# Uploaded media and generated exports go to the media_files and sync_files
# volumes. Set an adapter to s3 to use S3 instead, before files are stored:
# existing files are not moved. All five of its settings are then required; a
# missing one stops that service at boot with the setting named.

# Uploaded media.
# MEDIAS_FILES_ADAPTER=s3
# MEDIAS_FILES_BUCKET=
# MEDIAS_FILES_REGION=
# MEDIAS_FILES_ENDPOINT=
# MEDIAS_FILES_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# MEDIAS_FILES_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Generated exports.
# SYNC_FILES_ADAPTER=s3
# SYNC_FILES_BUCKET=
# SYNC_FILES_REGION=
# SYNC_FILES_ENDPOINT=
# SYNC_FILES_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# SYNC_FILES_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# === Logging ===============================================================

# info is the default. debug is noisy but useful when reporting a problem.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Returns error details in API responses. Keep off outside debugging.
# LOG_SHOW_ERROR_DETAILS=false

# === Error reporting =======================================================
# Your own Sentry project, if you use one. Empty disables reporting, which is
# the default: this install never reports to the vendor.

# The browser app. Nothing is built into the image, so this is the only way a
# DSN reaches it.
# SENTRY_DSN_FRONTEND=

# The backend services.
# SENTRY_DSN_IAM=
# SENTRY_DSN_MEDIA=
# SENTRY_DSN_DATASET=
# SENTRY_DSN_SETTING=
# SENTRY_DSN_AUDIT=
# SENTRY_DSN_SYNC=
# SENTRY_DSN_NOTIFICATION=

# Labels the browser app's events with the environment they came from.
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production

# Share of the browser app's page loads traced for performance.
# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=0.1

# Session replay records your users' screens. Off by default; turn it on only
# if your own Sentry project is an appropriate place for those recordings.
# SENTRY_REPLAY_SAMPLE_RATE=0
# SENTRY_REPLAY_ON_ERROR_SAMPLE_RATE=0
